A||||13||MEC91Firebird||S2000 vs. Fiero vs. FWD Imports||||3||
Z||000000||MEC91Firebird||02-12-2001||01:40 AM||||Okay, well, much to my suprise? Hondas S2000 is RWD, if you didnt know (I didnt know till recently), and i am plesently amused that when Honda wants a performance car they make it RWD.  Hmmmm, just kinda an intresting observation.  <BR>GM was so ahead of its time, the Fiero is the 80's Civic/Integra, except they got it right, RWD, Mid-engined.  Shame the Jap cars cant figure out where to put the engines and and drivetrain, there are so many performance parts for these cars right now, i would consider having one, except why start with something thats "broke" and try to fix it.  I will stick to the Fiero and F-bodys, and improve on something that was "right" to begin with!    <BR>Just my observations and rants.||||reg||3
Z||000001||1FST2M6||02-12-2001||08:20 AM||||the honda S600 and S1200 in 69 and 73 were both front engine RWD... the jones'n on the japanese market due to their lack of thought on the mid engine isn't a really a valid claim.. GM only made the Fiero mid engine... where Honda makes the NSX and the A-spec Del Sol in a mid engine configuration. (only 900 of the a-spec cars were ever made for a racing class - similar to the SCCA Neon and Miata) Nissan has always had front engine rwd sports cars so has Toyota. Mitsubishi however has never had an only RWD sports type car. they have been AWD for FWD. as for being right.. uum i'm not so sure the Fieros are "right".. grossly underpowered, weak steering geometry and not the best ergonomics. not saying that the NSX is any better.. it too has an open recall for every model year from 90-96 concerning thier conecting rod bearings... so we have a lot in common with some of them... I love my car but like every other car there is always one better.    <P>my $.02<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||02-12-2001||11:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>Mitsubishi however has never had an only RWD sports type car. they have been AWD for FWD. as for being right</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Don't forget the Mitsubishi Conquest from the mid to late 80's.  Rear wheel drive with independent suspension.  The Miata is a great handling rear drive car from Japan.  It seems intuitive that when the front wheels have to do the propulsion, steering, and majority of the braking, some ability would get lost, but those Preludes do pretty well.  My main complaint about the Fiero is the weight.  Would be nice if it lost about 500# but I still love to drive it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||02-12-2001||11:52 AM||||I don't see how you could say "the Fiero is the 80s Civic/Integra".  Those cars have impeccable reliability, and well, lets just say the Fiero isn't up there with it.  Not saying the Fiero isn't reliable, but I don't think a forum member here would say its as reliable or more reliable then a Civic/Integra.  Lets be realistic here, we're talking 80s GM vs. 21st century Honda....<P>Like someone mentioned the NSX, which is a Honda mid engined car.  And the Integra Type-R is a Honda sports car that is FWD.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||loafer87gt||02-12-2001||11:53 AM||||The S2000 looks like its not a very forgiving car. At SIR last year a guy had new S2000 and he did four runs that night, his best being 15.8. The first run he dumped the clutch at too high an RPM and sat there and spun, the second he lugged it and almost stalled, and the last time (the 15.8) he just barely chirped the tires and took off, but you could tell he wasn't in his powerband though. One thing that I was impressed with is an Auto-Honda V6 Accord that ran consistent 16.7 runs. Must kinda embarrassed the dude in the S2000.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||02-12-2001||12:18 PM||||Thats what most of the mags said about it loafer.  They all said how it was hard to drive and launch in general.  But they did much better then 15.8 in their trial runs.<BR>I think the S2000 redlines at like 9,000 RPMs?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
