Z||000010||Slammed Fiero||02-12-2001||11:28 PM||||My dad and I got to take one out (s2000) when he was looking for a new car , We found it very light on the rear end with a tendancy to come around. Excellent power, fit and finish. But the stock suspension set up didn't suit the short chassis of the car. As for the fiero being like a british sports car...too heavy...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||MEC91Firebird||02-12-2001||11:45 PM||||Okay, just to clarify my point, when i say the car is like the 80's version of the  Civic/Integra, I am just trying to make the point that it was a "commuter/econo" car that has become quite the tunner car.<P>As for the the british car comparison, RIGHT ON!!! My dad dislikes the Fiero as much as the MGB i wanted, however i like to point out he drove an MGB-GT when he was a Penn State student, however he never sways:-)<P>I guess i just have a fettish for "quirky" cars.||||reg||14
Z||000012||FieroLisa||02-13-2001||04:09 PM||||S2000: my only problem with the car is it has a real whiney honda sound. My dad just got one a year ago, after he test drove most of the other cars in its class...Basically everything the magazines say about it is true. Yes I believe it does redline around 9000, dont quite remember, and the power doesn't really kick in till about 4000. Its fun, though- one of these days I'll get to drive it! (my dad insists that I can't handle the power  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> haha little does he know )<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/pffsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||frontal lobe||02-13-2001||10:27 PM||||Good discussion!  I really admire the technology and accomplishment of the high-revving 4-cylinders, but in daily driving I would rarely get the use out of it because I don't have many places to take advantage of it.  I don't know what your speed would be in 2nd gear above 4000 rpm, but likely you would just be getting in the sweet spot of the power band and then just have to shut it down.  I used to run into that a lot when I had my mazda 2.2liter turbo.  So for the kind of driving I get to do 90+% of the time, I'd rather have big torque then horsepower.  <P>I guess I don't find a v6 Fiero GROSSLY under-powered for it's weight.  It may not be a head-snapper, but I find it pretty peppy.  I don't know about the 2.5.  I haven't driven one.<P>I agree the Fiero could lose a few pounds, but compared to cars built then and since, it's hard to find many cars lighter.  A major gripe of mine, and a major attraction for me to the Fiero.  I also have a Miata for my wife.  Many things I like about it (mainly weight, gas mileage, and handling related) but I hate driving it after I've just been driving the Fiero.  Where's the grunt??  Ans:  there isn't any.<P>And I agree that the japanese car company models generally have better repair records--yet many of those are actually built in the U.S.||||reg||1
